          "session_id": "full-mt-m-015",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is meant by secondary prevention?",
          "answer": "Secondary prevention in health care refers to measures that aim to identify and address a disease at an early stage, often before noticeable symptoms develop. The goal is to reduce the severity of the disease and prevent complications or worsening of the condition.\n\nKey components of secondary prevention include:\n\n1. **Screening**: This involves regular testing for diseases in individuals who do not yet have symptoms. Examples include mammograms for breast cancer, blood pressure checks for hypertension, and cholesterol screenings for heart disease.\n\n2. **Early Intervention**: When a condition is detected early through screening, interventions can be implemented to manage or mitigate the disease. This may involve medication, lifestyle changes, or follow-up monitoring.\n\n3. **Risk Factor Management**: Addressing modifiable risk factors, such as high blood pressure or high cholesterol, is also part of secondary prevention. This can help prevent the onset of more serious adverse health outcomes.\n\nBy implementing secondary prevention strategies, health care providers aim to catch diseases early, improve health outcomes, and potentially reduce health care costs associated with managing advanced stages of diseases (source: \"Prevention in health care decision making\"). \n\nIf you have specific aspects of secondary prevention you are curious about, feel free to ask!\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
